-- Q1: easy / L1 — Section 332 mandatory application
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 332, when a parent corporation owns at least 80% of a subsidiary's voting power and value, the tax-free liquidation treatment is:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Mandatory and cannot be avoided by election", "Elective at the parent's discretion", "Available only if the subsidiary has no accumulated E&P", "Dependent on IRS approval before the liquidation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 332 is mandatory when the 80% ownership threshold is met. The parent corporation cannot elect out of nonrecognition treatment, unlike many other tax-free provisions.
Wrong (B): Section 332 is not elective. Once the ownership threshold is satisfied, tax-free treatment applies automatically.
Wrong (C): Accumulated E&P has no bearing on whether Section 332 applies. The 80% ownership test is the sole qualification criterion.
Wrong (D): No IRS approval or ruling is required for Section 332 to apply. The statute operates by its terms when conditions are met.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: easy / L2 — Corporation's holding period for Section 351 contributed property
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Nolan contributes land held for four years (basis $80,000, FMV $150,000) to a newly formed corporation for 100% of its stock under Section 351. What is the corporation's holding period for the land?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Begins on the date of the transfer", "Includes Nolan's four-year holding period", "Begins on the date the corporation files its first tax return", "Is limited to one year regardless of Nolan's holding period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1223(2), when property is received in a Section 351 exchange and the transferor's basis carries over to the corporation under Section 362, the corporation's holding period includes the transferor's prior holding period.
Wrong (A): The holding period does not restart on the transfer date. The tacking rule under Section 1223(2) preserves the transferor's holding period.
Wrong (C): The filing of a tax return is unrelated to the holding period determination for contributed property.
Wrong (D): There is no one-year limitation. The full prior holding period tacks onto the corporation's holding period.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Section 338(h)(10) with S corporation target
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Buyer Corp acquires 100% of Target Inc., an S corporation, in a qualified stock purchase. Target has assets with a basis of $600,000 and FMV of $1,200,000. The selling shareholders have stock basis of $500,000. If the parties jointly elect Section 338(h)(10), how is the transaction reported?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The selling shareholders report a $700,000 capital gain on the stock sale", "Target recognizes $600,000 of gain on a deemed asset sale, which passes through to the selling shareholders on their final K-1s", "Buyer Corp recognizes $600,000 of gain on the deemed asset purchase", "Target recognizes $600,000 of gain and pays corporate-level tax before any pass-through"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 338(h)(10), the actual stock sale is disregarded. Target is treated as selling all assets at FMV in a deemed asset sale, recognizing $600,000 gain ($1,200,000 − $600,000). Because Target is an S corporation, this gain passes through to selling shareholders on their final K-1s, and Buyer gets a stepped-up FMV basis in the assets.
Wrong (A): The stock sale is disregarded under a 338(h)(10) election. Shareholders do not report a stock sale; they report the results of the deemed asset sale.
Wrong (B): While the gain amount and pass-through are correct, Buyer Corp does not recognize gain — it is the deemed purchaser that receives a stepped-up basis.
Wrong (D): S corporations generally do not pay corporate-level tax. The deemed sale gain passes through to shareholders under Subchapter S rules.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Partial liquidation 5-year active business safe harbor
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Fenwick Corp has operated a restaurant division and a catering division for eight years. Fenwick sells the restaurant division and distributes the proceeds to its individual shareholders. To qualify for partial liquidation treatment under Section 302(b)(4), which requirement must be satisfied?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The distribution must be made within one year of the sale", "The distribution must reduce the number of outstanding shares by at least 50%", "The shareholders must file an election with their individual tax returns", "The corporation must continue to actively conduct the catering division, which was conducted for at least five years before the distribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): IRC Section 302(e)(2) provides a safe harbor requiring that the distribution is attributable to the corporation ceasing a qualifying trade or business while continuing at least one other qualifying business. Both businesses must have been actively conducted for at least five years before the distribution.
Wrong (A): There is no one-year distribution requirement for partial liquidation treatment under Section 302(b)(4).
Wrong (B): There is no requirement that outstanding shares be reduced by any specific percentage. The test is applied at the corporate level.
Wrong (C): Partial liquidation treatment does not require a shareholder election. It is determined based on the nature of the distribution at the corporate level.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Section 362(e)(2) built-in loss limitation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Quinlan incorporates her sole proprietorship by contributing all assets to a new C corporation for 100% of the stock. One asset — a warehouse — has a $250,000 adjusted basis but only $180,000 FMV due to market decline. Section 362(e)(2) applies to the net built-in loss property. What basis does the corporation take in the warehouse?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$180,000, limited to FMV under the built-in loss duplication prevention rule", "$250,000, the transferor's full carryover basis regardless of FMV decline", "$215,000, computed as the midpoint of adjusted basis and current FMV", "$0, because built-in loss property cannot be contributed under Section 351"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 362(e)(2) limits the corporation's basis in contributed property to FMV when the property has a net built-in loss (basis exceeds FMV). This prevents the duplication of losses between the transferor's stock basis and the corporation's asset basis. The corporation's basis is $180,000.
Wrong (B): A full carryover basis of $250,000 would duplicate the built-in loss, which Section 362(e)(2) was enacted to prevent.
Wrong (C): There is no averaging method under Section 362(e)(2). The basis is limited to the lesser of adjusted basis or FMV.
Wrong (D): A zero basis is incorrect. The corporation takes a basis equal to the property's FMV when Section 362(e)(2) applies.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Section 381 tax attribute carryover in 332 liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Hawthorne Corp owns 100% of Millbrook Inc., which has $400,000 of net operating losses, $50,000 of unused general business credits, and $120,000 of accumulated E&P. Millbrook liquidates under Section 332. Hawthorne is evaluating what tax attributes transfer. Which outcome correctly reflects the carryover rules?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only the NOLs carry over; credits and E&P are forfeited", "NOLs, credits, and E&P all carry over to Hawthorne under Section 381", "NOLs and credits carry over, but E&P is eliminated in the liquidation", "All attributes carry over, but Section 382 eliminates the NOLs entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 381(a)(1), when a subsidiary liquidates under Section 332, the parent corporation succeeds to the subsidiary's tax attributes, including NOLs, tax credits, E&P, capital loss carryovers, and accounting methods.
Wrong (A): Section 381 provides for carryover of credits and E&P in addition to NOLs. All enumerated attributes in Section 381(c) transfer.
Wrong (C): E&P is specifically listed in Section 381(c)(2) as an attribute that carries over to the acquiring corporation.
Wrong (D): Section 382 may limit the use of NOLs following an ownership change, but a Section 332 liquidation of a wholly owned subsidiary does not itself trigger a Section 382 ownership change that eliminates the NOLs.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Section 707(a)(2)(B) disguised sale in partnership
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Prescott contributes land (basis $100,000, FMV $400,000) to a partnership for a 50% interest. Within 60 days, the partnership distributes $350,000 cash to Prescott. Under the disguised sale rules, how should the IRS likely recharacterize this transaction?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As a tax-free contribution followed by a tax-free distribution", "As a partial sale of the land — Prescott is treated as selling a portion for $350,000 and contributing the remainder", "As a fully taxable sale of the land for $400,000 with $350,000 paid immediately", "As ordinary income to Prescott equal to the $350,000 distribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 707(a)(2)(B), when a partner contributes property and receives a related distribution within two years (presumption period), the transactions are presumed to be a disguised sale. The IRS would recharacterize the contribution and distribution as a sale of the property for $350,000, and Prescott would recognize gain on the portion treated as sold.
Wrong (A): The proximity and relatedness of the contribution and distribution create a presumption of a disguised sale, overriding the general Section 721 nonrecognition rule.
Wrong (B): The disguised sale rules treat the transaction as a sale for the amount of the distribution ($350,000), not as a partial sale with a retained contributed portion.
Wrong (D): The gain would be characterized based on the nature of the property sold (capital gain if capital asset), not as ordinary income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Section 334(a) basis for non-parent shareholders in liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 334(a), what basis does a shareholder who is not a parent corporation take in property received in a complete liquidation under Section 331?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The corporation's adjusted basis (carryover basis)", "The shareholder's basis in the stock surrendered", "The lower of FMV or the corporation's adjusted basis", "Fair market value on the date of distribution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 334(a), a shareholder who receives property in a complete liquidation under Section 331 takes a basis in the property equal to its fair market value on the distribution date. This is consistent with the sale/exchange treatment of Section 331.
Wrong (A): Carryover basis applies under Section 334(b)(1) to a parent corporation in a Section 332 liquidation, not to non-parent shareholders.
Wrong (B): The stock basis determines the shareholder's gain or loss, but the basis in distributed property is FMV, not the stock basis.
Wrong (C): There is no lower-of rule for liquidating distributions to non-parent shareholders. The basis is simply FMV.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Short-term notes as boot in Section 351
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$In a Section 351 exchange, a transferor receives stock plus a short-term corporate note. The note is classified as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Boot, which may trigger gain recognition", "Qualified stock under the Section 351 nonrecognition rules", "A liability assumption under Section 357", "Tax-free additional consideration alongside the stock"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 351(a), nonrecognition applies only to stock received. Short-term notes, securities with a maturity of five years or less, and other non-stock property are classified as boot. Gain is recognized to the extent of boot received.
Wrong (B): Only stock of the transferee corporation qualifies for nonrecognition. Short-term notes are not treated as stock.
Wrong (C): A corporate note payable to the transferor is not a liability assumption under Section 357. It is new consideration issued by the corporation.
Wrong (D): Non-stock consideration is boot and may trigger gain recognition. It is not treated as tax-free.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Regular Section 338 vs 338(h)(10) strategic comparison
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Acquisitions Inc. is purchasing 100% of Target Corp (a C corporation) for $5,000,000. Target has assets with a basis of $2,000,000 and FMV of $5,000,000 and no liabilities. The selling shareholders have a stock basis of $1,500,000. A tax advisor is comparing a regular Section 338 election (buyer-only) versus a joint Section 338(h)(10) election. Which analysis correctly identifies the critical difference in tax burden?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Under both elections, only the buyer bears the deemed asset sale tax cost and selling shareholders report no gain", "Under 338(h)(10), the stock sale is disregarded and only the deemed asset sale is reported; under a regular 338, both the deemed asset sale and the shareholder stock sale are taxed, creating potential double taxation", "Under 338(h)(10), no corporate-level tax arises because the deemed sale is ignored; under a regular 338, the corporation pays tax on the deemed sale", "Under a regular 338, selling shareholders avoid all gain because the stock sale is disregarded and replaced by the deemed asset sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under a regular Section 338 election, the selling shareholders report their stock sale gain ($3,500,000), and the deemed asset sale generates corporate-level gain inside Target ($3,000,000), resulting in potential double taxation. Under Section 338(h)(10), the stock sale is disregarded, and only the deemed asset sale is reported by Target, avoiding the duplicative shareholder-level gain.
Wrong (A): Under a regular 338, the selling shareholders still report their stock sale gain separately, so the buyer does not bear the only tax cost.
Wrong (C): Under 338(h)(10), the deemed asset sale does create corporate-level tax for a C corporation target. The key advantage is eliminating double taxation, not eliminating all corporate tax.
Wrong (D): Under a regular 338, the selling shareholders still report their stock sale. The stock sale is only disregarded under 338(h)(10).$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Partial liquidation determined at corporate level
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Greystone Manufacturing distributes $500,000 from the sale of a division to its shareholders. One shareholder, Delaney, holds 10% of the stock and receives $50,000. Delaney's stock basis attributable to the redeemed shares is $20,000. Delaney asserts the distribution qualifies as a partial liquidation. At which level is the "not essentially equivalent to a dividend" test applied?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["At the individual shareholder level based on Delaney's ownership percentage", "At the partnership level if Delaney holds shares through a partnership", "At the corporate level, based on the nature of the distribution and the corporation's business activities", "At both the corporate and shareholder levels, requiring both tests to be satisfied"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 302(e)(1), a partial liquidation is determined at the corporate level, not the shareholder level. The test focuses on whether the distribution is attributable to a genuine contraction of the corporation's business activities.
Wrong (A): Unlike other Section 302 redemption tests (substantially disproportionate, meaningful reduction), the partial liquidation test is applied at the corporate level.
Wrong (B): The entity through which shares are held does not change the level at which the partial liquidation test is applied.
Wrong (D): Only the corporate-level test applies for partial liquidation. There is no additional shareholder-level test required.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Step transaction doctrine risk in Section 351
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Porter contributes property to a newly formed corporation for 100% of the stock under Section 351. Two weeks later, Porter sells 60% of the stock to an unrelated buyer pursuant to a pre-arranged agreement. If the IRS applies the step transaction doctrine, what is the most likely consequence?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 351 is unaffected because the stock sale is a separate transaction", "The subsequent sale is recharacterized as a partnership formation", "Porter's gain on the stock sale is converted from capital to ordinary", "The contribution and sale are collapsed into a single transaction, and Porter fails the 80% control requirement, making the entire transfer taxable"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the step transaction doctrine, the IRS may collapse the contribution and pre-arranged stock sale into a single integrated transaction. If Porter is treated as transferring property for only 40% of the stock (after the planned disposition), the 80% control test fails and the entire exchange is taxable.
Wrong (A): When a stock disposition is pre-arranged, the IRS can collapse the steps and treat the transferor as never having had control.
Wrong (B): Recharacterization as a partnership formation is not the consequence of a failed Section 351 exchange. The result is a taxable property transfer.
Wrong (C): The character of gain is not the issue. The step transaction doctrine challenges whether nonrecognition applies at all.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Section 721(b) investment partnership exception
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Five individuals contribute diversified stock portfolios to a newly formed partnership, each receiving a 20% interest. None of the individuals held more than 5% of any single company before contributing. After formation, the partnership holds a concentrated portfolio. Under Section 721(b), what is the tax treatment of the contributions?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Each partner recognizes gain on the contributed securities because the partnership qualifies as an investment company, and the transfers result in diversification", "The contributions are tax-free because Section 721 always applies to partnership formations", "The partnership recognizes gain on receipt of the securities under Section 723", "Each partner defers gain but must recognize it ratably over five years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 721(b) provides an exception to nonrecognition when appreciated securities are contributed to a partnership that would be treated as an investment company under Section 351(e). If the transfers result in diversification of the contributors' interests, each partner recognizes gain on the contributed securities.
Wrong (B): Section 721(b) is a specific statutory exception to the general nonrecognition rule of Section 721(a) for investment company partnerships.
Wrong (C): Partnerships do not recognize gain on receipt of contributed property. Gain recognition, if any, falls on the contributing partners.
Wrong (D): There is no ratable recognition provision. If Section 721(b) applies, the full built-in gain is recognized at the time of contribution.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — S corporation liquidation single-level tax advantage
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A CPA is advising the sole shareholder of Barrett Inc., an S corporation, on complete liquidation. Barrett holds equipment (basis $200,000, FMV $600,000) and the shareholder's stock basis is $250,000. The CPA is comparing the tax cost if Barrett were an S corporation versus a C corporation (assume 21% corporate rate and 20% capital gains rate for simplicity). Which analysis best reflects the structural tax advantage of S corporation status in liquidation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["There is no structural difference because both entity types recognize corporate-level gain under Section 336 in the same manner", "The S corporation's deemed sale gain passes through to the shareholder, increasing stock basis and eliminating shareholder-level gain — single tax; the C corporation pays entity-level tax plus shareholder capital gains tax — double tax", "The S corporation avoids all gain recognition because S corporations are fully exempt from Section 336 deemed sale rules", "The C corporation produces lower total tax because the corporate tax reduces the liquidating distribution amount, creating a smaller shareholder gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 336, both S and C corporations recognize gain on asset distributions. However, the S corporation's gain passes through to the shareholder (increasing stock basis from $250,000 to $650,000), so the liquidating distribution at FMV produces no additional shareholder gain. The C corporation pays $84,000 corporate tax, reduces the distribution to $516,000, and the shareholder pays additional capital gains tax, resulting in double taxation.
Wrong (A): While both entity types recognize corporate-level gain under Section 336, the pass-through treatment for S corporations creates a significant structural difference in total tax burden.
Wrong (C): S corporations are not exempt from Section 336. They recognize gain on liquidating distributions just like C corporations, but the gain passes through to shareholders.
Wrong (D): While the corporate tax reduces the distribution amount, the total combined tax burden is higher for the C corporation due to double taxation. A smaller shareholder gain does not offset the corporate tax already paid.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Accounts receivable as property under Section 351
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Whitfield, a cash-basis accountant, contributes $30,000 in unrealized accounts receivable (zero tax basis) and $20,000 cash to a newly formed corporation for 100% of the stock. Under Section 351, the accounts receivable are treated as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Services, which disqualify Whitfield from the control group", "An obligation that requires immediate income recognition by Whitfield", "Property eligible for nonrecognition treatment under Section 351", "Boot received by the corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 351, accounts receivable are classified as property. Even zero-basis receivables of a cash-basis taxpayer qualify for nonrecognition treatment. The corporation will recognize income when it collects the receivables under the assignment of income doctrine applied through Section 351(a).
Wrong (A): Accounts receivable are property, not services. Only the performance of services is excluded from the definition of property under Section 351.
Wrong (B): No immediate income recognition is required for the transferor. Section 351 defers recognition; the income shifts to the corporation upon collection.
Wrong (D): Boot is property received by the transferor from the corporation. The accounts receivable are property contributed to the corporation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: hard / L3 — Section 336(d)(1) built-in loss property to related party
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Harmon Corp is liquidating and distributes two properties to its sole shareholder, Greene (a related party). Property X was contributed by Greene three years ago (current basis $300,000, FMV $200,000, same values at contribution). Property Y has been held by Harmon for ten years (basis $150,000, FMV $100,000). Under Section 336(d), what loss may Harmon recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0 on Property X and $0 on Property Y", "$100,000 loss on Property X and $50,000 loss on Property Y", "$0 on Property X and $50,000 loss on Property Y, totaling $50,000", "$100,000 loss on Property X and $0 on Property Y"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 336(d)(1)(A), loss is disallowed on distributions to related persons if the property was acquired by the corporation in a Section 351 transaction (or as a contribution to capital) within the five-year period ending on the distribution date and had a built-in loss at the time of contribution. Property X was contributed by Greene three years ago with a built-in loss, so the $100,000 loss is disallowed. Property Y was held for ten years and was not contributed within five years, so the $50,000 loss is recognized.
Wrong (A): Loss on Property Y is not disallowed because it was not contributed within the five-year lookback period.
Wrong (B): Loss on Property X is disallowed under Section 336(d)(1)(A) because it was contributed by a related party with a built-in loss within five years.
Wrong (C): This reverses the correct treatment. Property X's loss is disallowed, and Property Y's loss is allowed.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Section 332 timing requirement
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Caldwell Corp owns 100% of Hayes Inc. and adopts a plan to liquidate Hayes. The liquidation takes 18 months to complete. Under Section 332, what timing requirement must the liquidating distributions satisfy for tax-free treatment to apply?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All distributions must occur within one taxable year of the adoption of the plan or within three years from the close of the taxable year in which the first distribution is made", "All distributions must be completed within 12 months of the first distribution regardless of taxable years", "The plan must be completed within two years of adoption, with IRS approval for extensions", "There is no timing requirement as long as the 80% ownership test is met at the time of adoption"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 332(b) provides two timing alternatives: (1) the subsidiary transfers all property in one taxable year (Section 332(b)(2)), or (2) distributions are made pursuant to a plan of liquidation and completed within three years from the close of the taxable year in which the first distribution is made (Section 332(b)(3)).
Wrong (B): The three-year period runs from the close of the taxable year of the first distribution, not from the date of the first distribution. Additionally, the one-taxable-year alternative is also available.
Wrong (C): The statutory period is three years, not two years, and no IRS approval is required.
Wrong (D): A specific timing requirement exists under Section 332(b). Distributions that extend beyond the statutory period may disqualify the liquidation from Section 332 treatment.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Accommodation transferor in Section 351
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Crawford contributes equipment (basis $40,000, FMV $300,000) for 75% of a new corporation's stock. To satisfy the 80% control test, Crawford arranges for Beck to contribute $1,000 cash for a 5% interest. The remaining 20% is issued to Martin for services. The IRS examines whether Beck's contribution is genuine. If Beck is treated as an accommodation transferor, what is the consequence?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Martin's services contribution becomes tax-free because the transaction is recharacterized", "Beck is excluded from the transferor group, so Crawford alone holds 75% — below the 80% control threshold — making the entire exchange taxable", "Martin is reclassified as a property transferor, which cures the control deficiency for Crawford", "The corporation's basis in the equipment is stepped up to FMV regardless of Section 351 qualification"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): If Beck is deemed an accommodation transferor whose contribution lacks independent economic substance, the IRS excludes Beck from the transferor group. Without Beck, Crawford alone holds 75%, failing the 80% control requirement. The entire exchange becomes taxable to Crawford.
Wrong (A): Martin's services contribution is always taxable regardless of Section 351. Excluding an accommodation transferor does not change services treatment.
Wrong (C): Martin contributed services, not property. Reclassifying Martin as a property transferor is not permissible under Section 351.
Wrong (D): A stepped-up FMV basis would be a consequence of failing Section 351, not a direct result of the accommodation transferor determination.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Section 736(a) vs 736(b) based on partnership agreement
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Nelson retires from a general partnership. The partnership agreement does not provide for payments for goodwill. The partnership makes payments of $300,000, of which $200,000 is for Nelson's share of tangible partnership property and $100,000 is attributable to goodwill. How are the $100,000 goodwill payments classified?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As Section 736(b) payments treated as distributions for Nelson's partnership interest", "As capital gain to Nelson under the installment sale rules", "As Section 736(a) payments — either guaranteed payments (if fixed) or distributive share (if based on income) — deductible by the partnership", "As return of capital to the extent of Nelson's outside basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 736(a), when the partnership agreement does not provide for goodwill payments, amounts paid to a retiring general partner for unstated goodwill are treated as Section 736(a) payments. These are either guaranteed payments (if a fixed amount) or a distributive share of partnership income, and are deductible or reduce the remaining partners' shares.
Wrong (A): Section 736(b) covers payments for the partner's share of partnership property, but unstated goodwill is excluded from Section 736(b) for general partners when the agreement is silent.
Wrong (B): The installment sale rules do not apply to Section 736(a) payments. These payments are treated as ordinary income or a distributive share.
Wrong (D): Return of capital treatment applies to Section 736(b) payments, not to Section 736(a) payments for unstated goodwill.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Installment obligations in corporate liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Thornton Corp adopts a plan of complete liquidation and distributes an installment note (face $300,000, remaining gain of $180,000) to its sole shareholder, Prescott. Thornton had been deferring gain under Section 453. Upon distributing the installment note in liquidation, what is the tax consequence to Thornton Corp?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Thornton continues to defer gain until Prescott collects on the note", "Prescott recognizes the $180,000 gain immediately upon receiving the note", "Thornton may assign the installment obligation to Prescott with no acceleration of gain", "Thornton must recognize the $180,000 deferred gain under Section 453B because the distribution is a disposition of the installment obligation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 453B(a), any disposition of an installment obligation — including a distribution in liquidation — triggers recognition of the deferred gain. Thornton Corp must recognize the remaining $180,000 gain on the distribution. However, Section 453(h) may allow the shareholder (Prescott) to continue installment reporting on the note received.
Wrong (A): The corporation cannot continue to defer gain after it distributes the installment obligation. Section 453B accelerates recognition upon disposition.
Wrong (B): The immediate recognition falls on the corporation distributing the note, not on the shareholder receiving it. The shareholder may qualify for installment treatment under Section 453(h).
Wrong (C): A liquidating distribution of an installment obligation is a disposition under Section 453B, which does accelerate gain at the corporate level.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Section 357(b) tax avoidance purpose analysis
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A CPA is reviewing a Section 351 transaction where Marchetti contributes property (basis $500,000, FMV $800,000) to a corporation for stock. Before the transfer, Marchetti encumbers the property with a $400,000 loan and uses the proceeds for personal expenses unrelated to the business. The IRS argues that under Section 357(b), the liability assumption was tax-motivated. If the IRS prevails, what is the tax consequence compared to normal Section 357(a) treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Under Section 357(b), the entire $400,000 liability is treated as boot, causing Marchetti to recognize $300,000 of gain (the realized gain); under normal 357(a), the liability assumption is not boot and no gain is recognized", "Under Section 357(b), Marchetti recognizes $400,000 of ordinary income; under normal 357(a), the liability reduces stock basis but causes no gain", "Under Section 357(b), the transaction is voided and Marchetti retains the property; under 357(a), the transfer proceeds tax-free", "Under Section 357(b), only the portion of the liability exceeding basis triggers gain ($0 here since $400,000 < $500,000); under 357(a), same result"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 357(b), if the principal purpose of the liability assumption is tax avoidance or lacks a bona fide business purpose, ALL assumed liabilities are treated as boot. Marchetti's realized gain is $300,000 ($800,000 − $500,000), and boot of $400,000 triggers gain recognition of $300,000 (lesser of boot and realized gain). Under normal Section 357(a) treatment, the liability assumption is ignored and no gain results.
Wrong (B): The gain is recognized at the lesser of boot received and realized gain, not the full liability amount. The character follows the underlying property, not automatic ordinary income treatment.
Wrong (C): Section 357(b) does not void the transaction. The Section 351 exchange still occurs, but gain is recognized because the liabilities are recharacterized as boot.
Wrong (D): Section 357(b) treats the entire liability as boot, not just the excess over basis. The excess-over-basis rule is Section 357(c), which is a separate provision.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — No loss on property-only partnership liquidating distribution
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 731(a)(2), when may a partner recognize a loss on a liquidating distribution from a partnership?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Whenever the FMV of distributed property is less than the partner's outside basis", "Only when the distribution consists solely of cash, unrealized receivables, and/or inventory, and the total is less than the partner's outside basis", "Whenever the partnership has an overall net loss for the taxable year", "Only when the partner has held the partnership interest for more than one year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 731(a)(2), a partner may recognize a loss on a liquidating distribution only when no property other than cash, unrealized receivables, or inventory is received, and the amount distributed is less than the partner's outside basis.
Wrong (A): Receiving property with FMV below outside basis does not trigger loss recognition. The partner takes a substituted basis in the property instead.
Wrong (C): Partnership operating losses are allocated to partners on their K-1s and are unrelated to the loss recognition rules for liquidating distributions.
Wrong (D): The holding period of the partnership interest is irrelevant to whether loss is recognized on a liquidating distribution.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Recapture potential does not trigger gain at Section 351 formation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 351, when a transferor contributes depreciable property with potential Section 1245 recapture to a corporation, the recapture at the time of transfer is:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognized immediately as ordinary income by the transferor", "Eliminated permanently because the corporation receives a new depreciable asset", "Deferred — the recapture potential carries over to the corporation under Section 1245(b)(3)", "Converted to capital gain character for the transferor"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1245(b)(3), when property is transferred in a nonrecognition transaction such as Section 351, the recapture potential is not triggered. Instead, the corporation inherits the recapture potential and will recognize ordinary income upon a future taxable disposition.
Wrong (A): Section 1245 recapture is not triggered in a Section 351 exchange because no gain is recognized on the transfer of property for stock.
Wrong (B): The recapture potential is not eliminated. It carries over to the corporation and attaches to the transferred property.
Wrong (D): Recapture potential maintains its ordinary income character. It is not converted to capital gain at any point.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Section 332 requires 80% ownership throughout liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Waverly Corp owns 90% of Dalton Inc. and adopts a plan of liquidation. During the liquidation, Waverly sells 15% of its Dalton stock to a third party, reducing its ownership to 75%. Dalton then completes its liquidating distributions. Under Section 332, what is the tax consequence to Waverly?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 332 applies because Waverly owned 80% at the time the plan was adopted", "Section 332 applies to the first distributions made while Waverly held 90%, and Section 331 applies to later distributions", "Waverly must recognize gain or loss under Section 331 on all distributions because it failed to maintain 80% ownership throughout the liquidation", "Section 332 still applies because Waverly held more than 50% throughout the liquidation period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 332 requires the parent to own at least 80% of the subsidiary's stock from the date of adoption of the plan through receipt of all liquidating distributions. Because Waverly dropped to 75% during the liquidation period, Section 332 does not apply, and Waverly must recognize gain or loss under Section 331.
Wrong (A): Meeting the 80% threshold only at adoption is insufficient. The parent must maintain 80% ownership throughout the entire liquidation period.
Wrong (B): Section 332 is an all-or-nothing provision. It does not apply to some distributions and not others within the same liquidation.
Wrong (D): A 50% threshold does not exist under Section 332. The statute requires 80% ownership maintained throughout the liquidation period.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L2 — Section 351 nonvoting stock in the control calculation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Harper contributes property for 85% of a corporation's voting stock but only 70% of its single class of nonvoting preferred stock. The remaining interests are held by unrelated parties who did not participate in the exchange. Under Section 368(c), does Harper satisfy the control requirement for Section 351?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because control requires 80% of voting power AND 80% of each nonvoting class, and Harper holds only 70% of the nonvoting class", "Yes, because Harper holds more than 80% of the voting stock, which alone satisfies the control test", "Yes, because Harper's total combined ownership across all classes exceeds the 80% threshold", "No, because Section 351 requires 100% ownership of all stock classes to qualify for nonrecognition"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 368(c), control for Section 351 purposes requires ownership of at least 80% of total voting power AND at least 80% of the total number of shares of each class of nonvoting stock. Harper fails the nonvoting stock prong (70% < 80%), so the control requirement is not met.
Wrong (B): Meeting only the voting stock threshold is insufficient. Both prongs of the control test must be satisfied simultaneously.
Wrong (C): There is no combined or blended ownership test. Each prong — voting and nonvoting — must independently meet the 80% threshold.
Wrong (D): The threshold is 80%, not 100%. However, both the voting power and each nonvoting class must independently meet 80%.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Partial liquidation not available to corporate shareholders
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Under Section 302(b)(4), partial liquidation treatment providing sale/exchange treatment is available only to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both corporate and noncorporate shareholders", "Noncorporate shareholders", "Corporate shareholders owning less than 50%", "Shareholders who have held their stock for more than one year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 302(b)(4), partial liquidation sale/exchange treatment is limited to noncorporate shareholders. Corporate shareholders receiving distributions in a partial liquidation are treated under the normal dividend rules of Section 301.
Wrong (A): The statute explicitly limits partial liquidation treatment to noncorporate shareholders. Corporate shareholders are excluded.
Wrong (C): No corporate shareholders qualify for partial liquidation treatment, regardless of their ownership percentage.
Wrong (D): The holding period of the stock is not a requirement for partial liquidation treatment. The limitation is based on the shareholder's status as noncorporate.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Section 351 multiparty — one person's treatment doesn't invalidate others
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Three individuals form a corporation. Allen contributes equipment (basis $40,000, FMV $100,000) for 40% of the stock plus $10,000 cash boot. Baker contributes cash of $100,000 for 40% of the stock. Carter contributes property (basis $30,000, FMV $50,000) for 20% of the stock. Together the transferors hold 100%. Allen receives boot. How does Allen's receipt of boot affect Baker and Carter?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All three transferors must recognize gain because one received boot", "Baker and Carter must also recognize gain proportional to the boot Allen received", "Baker and Carter qualify for full nonrecognition — only Allen recognizes gain to the extent of boot received; the receipt of boot by one transferor does not disqualify the others", "The transaction fails Section 351 because boot was paid to a transferor"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 351(b), boot triggers gain recognition only to the transferor who receives it. Baker and Carter received solely stock and qualify for full nonrecognition. The payment of boot to one member of the transferor group does not affect the tax treatment of others.
Wrong (A): Gain recognition under Section 351(b) is determined on a transferor-by-transferor basis. The group collectively meeting the control test is sufficient.
Wrong (B): There is no proportional gain sharing among transferors. Only the person receiving boot recognizes gain.
Wrong (D): Payment of boot does not disqualify the entire Section 351 transaction. The exchange still qualifies, but the boot recipient recognizes gain.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Section 453(h) installment reporting for shareholders in liquidation
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Birchwood Corp adopts a plan of complete liquidation and sells its assets for $800,000, receiving a $500,000 installment note and $300,000 cash. Birchwood distributes both the note and the cash to its sole shareholder, Manning, whose stock basis is $350,000. Under Section 453(h), how may Manning report the gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Manning must recognize the entire $450,000 gain in the year of the liquidating distribution", "Manning may not use installment reporting because the corporation already triggered Section 453B", "Manning reports the $300,000 cash as a return of capital and recognizes no gain until the note is collected", "Manning may treat the installment note as if Manning had directly sold the assets, reporting gain ratably as note payments are collected under Section 453(h)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 453(h), a shareholder receiving qualifying installment obligations in a complete liquidation within a 12-month period may treat the obligations as if the shareholder directly made the sale. This allows the shareholder to report gain ratably as payments are received on the installment note.
Wrong (A): Section 453(h) specifically permits the shareholder to defer gain recognition on installment obligations received in a qualifying liquidation.
Wrong (B): While Section 453B applies at the corporate level upon distribution, Section 453(h) provides a separate rule allowing the shareholder to use installment reporting.
Wrong (C): The $300,000 cash is part of the total liquidating distribution and must be included in the gain calculation for the year received, not treated purely as return of capital.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Section 351 with boot vs Section 721 partnership formation with debt
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$A tax advisor is counseling Mercer, who plans to contribute property (basis $200,000, FMV $500,000, subject to a $250,000 liability) to a business entity for a 100% ownership interest. If formed as a corporation under Section 351, Mercer would recognize $50,000 gain under Section 357(c) (liabilities exceed basis). If formed as a partnership, Mercer's share of the $250,000 liability would increase outside basis. Which analysis correctly evaluates the formation alternatives?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The partnership formation avoids gain recognition because the partner's share of partnership liabilities increases outside basis, preventing the liabilities-exceed-basis problem that triggers Section 357(c) gain in a corporate formation — making the partnership structure preferable when encumbered property has low basis", "The corporate formation is preferable because the $50,000 gain creates a higher stock basis, providing a larger deduction on future sale", "Both structures produce identical tax results because liabilities are treated as boot in both corporate and partnership formations", "The partnership formation triggers more gain because partners must recognize the full liability amount as income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 721 and Section 752, a partner's share of partnership liabilities increases outside basis, so the liabilities-exceed-basis problem that triggers gain under corporate Section 357(c) does not arise in a partnership context. This is a fundamental structural advantage of partnership formation for encumbered, low-basis property.
Wrong (B): While the $50,000 gain increases stock basis somewhat, the forced current recognition of gain is generally disadvantageous compared to full deferral in a partnership structure.
Wrong (C): Liabilities are not treated as boot in partnership formations. Section 752 provides a fundamentally different mechanism — liability sharing increases partner basis.
Wrong (D): Partners do not recognize the full liability as income. Partnership liabilities increase outside basis and generally do not trigger gain recognition.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Section 338(h)(10) benefit analysis for S corp targets
(6, 'Entity Formation and Liquidation', $EXPL$Keystone Holdings is acquiring 100% of Pinnacle Services, an S corporation, for $4,000,000. Pinnacle's assets include depreciable equipment (basis $500,000, FMV $1,200,000), goodwill (basis $0, FMV $1,800,000), and inventory (basis $600,000, FMV $1,000,000). The selling shareholders have a combined stock basis of $1,100,000. A tax advisor is evaluating whether a Section 338(h)(10) election benefits the buyer. Which factor provides the primary advantage to Keystone as the buyer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The election allows Keystone to avoid paying the full purchase price by reducing the tax cost", "Keystone receives a stepped-up FMV basis in all assets, creating depreciable and amortizable deductions not available in a straight stock purchase", "The election eliminates all tax liability for the selling S corporation shareholders on their final K-1s", "Keystone can immediately expense the full $4,000,000 purchase price as a deduction in the acquisition year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): The primary buyer advantage of a Section 338(h)(10) election is the stepped-up basis in the target's assets. In a straight stock purchase, Keystone would take a cost basis in the stock but the inside asset bases remain unchanged ($500,000 equipment, $0 goodwill, $600,000 inventory). With the election, Keystone gets FMV basis in all assets, creating significant future depreciation and amortization deductions.
Wrong (A): The election does not change the purchase price. Keystone still pays $4,000,000 for the business.
Wrong (C): The election does not eliminate tax for selling shareholders. The deemed asset sale gain passes through to them on their final S corporation K-1s.
Wrong (D): The stepped-up basis creates depreciation and amortization deductions over the applicable recovery periods, not an immediate full deduction of the purchase price.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;